  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nGOES water vapor imagery continues to depict an Omega-like blocking \npattern with a stout ridge over the central CONUS bookended by deep \nupper level troughing off the Mid-Atlantic coast and over western \nCanada. At the surface, RAP analysis shows sprawling high pressure \npersisting over much of the Great Lakes Basin and eastern Canada. \nThe result is clear skies over Upper Michigan early this morning \nwith temperatures dropping well down into the 40s across much of the \narea. Patchy fog development is being observed in many locations, \nespecially across the central UP. Will continue to monitor \nvisibility trends through the rest of the morning to see if any \nheadlines become necessary.\n\nThe combination of the upper level blocking pattern and surface high \npressure will more or less maintain the status quo today through \nMonday, yielding mostly dry weather as highs climb into the upper \n60s to low 70s across the area each day. Still could see a few \nshowers graze the far western UP as a weak shortwave rides up the \nridge late tonight into Sunday, but impacts remain minimal as \nguidance keeps any measurable rain confined to Gogebic and Ontonagon \nCounties, with the latest HREF depicting a 30% chance for as much as \na tenth of an inch of rain around Ironwood. Otherwise, seasonably \nwarm temps and good mixing out of dewpoints will continue to yield \nRH values dipping to around 40% if not a bit lower in interior areas \neach afternoon, but the weak pressure gradient and resulting light \nwinds will continue to limit any fire weather concerns.\n\nA pattern change still appears likely by the middle of next week as \nhigh pressure finally breaks down and opens the door to upper level \ntroughing and subsequent cooler and wetter weather across the \nregion. Although plenty of spread remains, guidance has at least \nstarted to come into general agreement on some of the large scale \ndetails, bringing a swath of rain across the UP sometime late \nTuesday through early Wednesday. Will have to wait on some of the \nfiner scale details to come into place before starting to get too \nfar into potential rain amounts, but NBM probabilities for at least \na half inch have come down slightly to 20-30% across the area in the \npast few runs.",
